From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David Connolly <david@connol.ly>
Date: Thu, 06 Feb 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] cpu/intel/model_206ax: add configurable ACPI C-state
 limit

The ACPI _CST table advertises available core C-states to the
operating system.  Currently, the three _CST slots (acpi_c1,
acpi_c2, acpi_c3) must be configured per-board in the devicetree.
If unset (all zero), no _CST object is generated and the OS falls
back to its built-in table (Linux intel_idle ignores _CST entirely
and always uses its own MWAIT hint table).

Add a Kconfig int ACPI_CSTATE_LIMIT that auto-populates the _CST
slots with a standard mapping when the devicetree does not set them
explicitly.  This provides a simple single-knob control for Windows
and other OSes that honour ACPI _CST:

  0 = no _CST generated (current default, OS uses built-in)
  1 = C1 only
  2 = C1 + C3  (core C3: L1/L2 flush, clocks stopped)
  3 = C1 + C3 + C6  (core power gated)
  4 = C1 + C3 + C7  (C6 + LLC flush)

Explicit devicetree acpi_c1/c2/c3 values always take precedence
over the Kconfig default.

Note for Linux users: the intel_idle driver bypasses ACPI _CST and
uses its own built-in C-state table.  To limit core C-states on
Linux, use the kernel parameter intel_idle.max_cstate=N.

Change-Id: I0000000000000000000000000000000000000013
Signed-off-by: David Connolly <david@connol.ly>
---
 src/cpu/intel/model_206ax/Kconfig | 24 ++++++++++++++++++++++++
 src/cpu/intel/model_206ax/acpi.c  | 22 ++++++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 46 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/cpu/intel/model_206ax/Kconfig b/src/cpu/intel/model_206ax/Kconfig
index 4444444444..5555555555 100644
--- a/src/cpu/intel/model_206ax/Kconfig
+++ b/src/cpu/intel/model_206ax/Kconfig
@@ -72,4 +72,28 @@ config ENERGY_PERF_BIAS
 	  Use 0 for overclocked desktops.  Note that Linux may
 	  override this via the intel_epb driver at OS boot.
 
+config ACPI_CSTATE_LIMIT
+	int "Maximum ACPI C-state advertised to OS"
+	default 0
+	range 0 4
+	help
+	  Auto-populate the ACPI _CST table with a standard core
+	  C-state mapping up to the specified depth.  This controls
+	  what core C-states the OS may request via MWAIT.
+
+	    0 = no _CST table (OS uses built-in defaults)
+	    1 = C1 only
+	    2 = C1 + C3  (L1/L2 flush, clocks stopped; 63us exit)
+	    3 = C1 + C3 + C6  (core power gated; 87us exit)
+	    4 = C1 + C3 + C7  (C6 + LLC flush; 90us exit)
+
+	  Explicit devicetree acpi_c1/c2/c3 values take precedence.
+
+	  This is effective for Windows and OSes that honour ACPI
+	  _CST.  Linux intel_idle bypasses _CST entirely; use the
+	  kernel parameter intel_idle.max_cstate=N instead.
+
+	  Use 1 for maximum stability on overclocked desktops.
+	  Use 0 (or leave devicetree unset) for default behaviour.
+
 endif # CPU_INTEL_MODEL_206AX
diff --git a/src/cpu/intel/model_206ax/acpi.c b/src/cpu/intel/model_206ax/acpi.c
index aaaaaaaaaa..bbbbbbbbbb 100644
--- a/src/cpu/intel/model_206ax/acpi.c
+++ b/src/cpu/intel/model_206ax/acpi.c
@@ -117,6 +117,28 @@ static void generate_C_state_entries(const struct device *dev)
 
 	int acpi_cstates[3] = { conf->acpi_c1, conf->acpi_c2, conf->acpi_c3 };
 
+	/*
+	 * If the devicetree does not set any C-states explicitly, populate
+	 * a standard mapping from the ACPI_CSTATE_LIMIT Kconfig value.
+	 *
+	 * Mapping:
+	 *   limit >= 1 → ACPI C1 = hardware C1
+	 *   limit >= 2 → ACPI C2 = hardware C3 (core clocks stopped)
+	 *   limit >= 3 → ACPI C3 = hardware C6 (core power gated)
+	 *   limit >= 4 → ACPI C3 = hardware C7 (C6 + LLC flush)
+	 */
+	if (CONFIG_ACPI_CSTATE_LIMIT > 0 &&
+	    acpi_cstates[0] == 0 && acpi_cstates[1] == 0 && acpi_cstates[2] == 0) {
+		acpi_cstates[0] = C_STATE_C1;
+		if (CONFIG_ACPI_CSTATE_LIMIT >= 2)
+			acpi_cstates[1] = C_STATE_C3;
+		if (CONFIG_ACPI_CSTATE_LIMIT >= 3)
+			acpi_cstates[2] = C_STATE_C6;
+		if (CONFIG_ACPI_CSTATE_LIMIT >= 4)
+			acpi_cstates[2] = C_STATE_C7;
+		printk(BIOS_INFO, "ACPI C-state limit %d: auto-populated _CST\n",
+		       CONFIG_ACPI_CSTATE_LIMIT);
+	}
+
 	acpi_cstate_t acpi_cstate_map[ARRAY_SIZE(acpi_cstates)] = { 0 };
 	/* Count number of active C-states */
 	int count = 0;
-- 
